  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
    "lesson_core_move": "Express a same-frequency sinusoidal sum in amplitude-phase form by adding component phasors and converting the resultant magnitude and angle into the combined amplitude and phase.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A same-frequency sinusoidal sum should be read as a single cosine of the form $A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$ after the component phasors are added. This lesson is about turning the sum into that one cosine and reading off the combined amplitude and phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_1\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi_1)+A_2\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi_2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not add the amplitudes and phases directly. Keep the shared $\\omega$ fixed, convert each cosine to its phasor, and use phasor addition as the only combination step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The phasor bridge is the same for each term: a cosine becomes a phasor with the same magnitude and angle, and those phasors add as vectors.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_1\\angle\\phi_1 + A_2\\angle\\phi_2 = A\\angle\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the base two-cosine case, the resultant phasor gives the answer: its magnitude becomes the new amplitude and its angle becomes the new phase. Later examples will keep the same angular frequency and add only one new wrinkle to this phasor-to-cosine loop.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reusable rule is simple: hold the common $\\omega$ fixed, add the component phasors, and read the resultant magnitude and angle as the single cosine's amplitude and phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Combining Two Same-Frequency Cosines into One Cosine",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Combine $4\\cos(8t+\\pi/3)$ and $2\\cos(8t-\\pi/6)$ into one cosine in amplitude-phase form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both terms share the same angular frequency $8$, so the right move is to convert each cosine to a phasor, add the phasors, and then read the resultant magnitude and angle back as one cosine.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4\\cos(8t+\\pi/3)+2\\cos(8t-\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convert each cosine to a phasor first. Then add the phasors in rectangular form so the shared frequency stays fixed and the arithmetic stays component-wise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n4\\angle\\frac{\\pi}{3} &= 2 + j2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n2\\angle\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\sqrt{3} - j \\\\\n4\\angle\\frac{\\pi}{3} + 2\\angle\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) &= (2+\\sqrt{3}) + j(2\\sqrt{3}-1)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The resultant phasor has magnitude $2\\sqrt{5}$, and its angle is the quadrant-I angle of $(2+\\sqrt{3})+j(2\\sqrt{3}-1)$. That magnitude and angle are the new amplitude and phase of the combined cosine.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\sqrt{5}\\cos\\left(8t+\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-1}{2+\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the complete loop is: keep the common $\\omega$ unchanged, add the component phasors, and convert the resultant magnitude and angle back into one cosine.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Resultant Amplitude and Phase from Component Phasors",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "The component phasors are $3+j4$ and $2-j1$. Find the amplitude and phase of the combined sinusoid.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The component phasors are already in rectangular form, so the first move is to add the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n(3+j4) + (2-j1) &= (3+2) + j(4-1) \\\\\n&= 5+j3\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That rectangular result is the resultant phasor. Because both parts are positive, the angle is the quadrant-I angle of the point $(5,3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5+j3 = \\sqrt{5^2+3^2}\\angle\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) = \\sqrt{34}\\angle\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the combined sinusoid has amplitude $\\sqrt{34}$ and phase $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)$ at the shared angular frequency. Later examples can reuse the same rectangular-addition workflow and only change the component values.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Keeping the Correct Quadrant for the Resultant Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert the phasor sum $-2+j3$ into the amplitude-phase form of the corresponding cosine.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the rectangular phasor $-2+j3$. Treat it as a vector: first find its magnitude, then choose the phase from the signs of the real and imaginary parts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|Z| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{13}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the absolute values to get the reference angle. That gives $\\theta_{\\text{ref}} = \\tan^{-1}(3/2)$, but this is not the final phase yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-2 < 0,\\; 3 > 0 \\Rightarrow \\theta \\text{ is in Quadrant II } \\Rightarrow \\theta = \\pi - \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phasor is $\\sqrt{13}\\angle\\left(\\pi - \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)$, and the corresponding cosine is $x(t)=\\sqrt{13}\\cos\\left(\\omega t+\\pi-\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Amplitude-Phase Form of a Sinusoidal Sum",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Combining More Than Two Same-Frequency Cosines",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Combine $2\\cos(6t)+2\\cos(6t+\\pi/3)+2\\cos(6t+2\\pi/3)$ into one cosine in amplitude-phase form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is three same-frequency cosines at angular frequency $6$. Put each term into a phasor, add the phasors, and then convert the result back to amplitude-phase form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Z = 2\\angle 0 + 2\\angle \\frac{\\pi}{3} + 2\\angle \\frac{2\\pi}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convert each phasor to rectangular form so the real and imaginary parts combine directly. Here the $1$ and $-1$ real parts cancel, which makes the sum easy to read.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Z = (2+j0) + \\left(1+j\\sqrt{3}\\right) + \\left(-1+j\\sqrt{3}\\right) = 2 + j2\\sqrt{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now read off the magnitude and phase from the rectangular sum. Since $Z$ is in Quadrant I, the principal angle is the correct angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|Z| = \\sqrt{2^2 + (2\\sqrt{3})^2} = 4,\\qquad \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3},\\qquad x(t)=4\\cos\\left(6t+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the three cosines combine to one cosine at the same frequency $6$, with amplitude $4$ and phase $\\pi/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
